<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ziauddin Barani</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1285">1285</a>–<a href="/years/1357">1357</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ziauddin Barani was a political thinker and historian of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a>, best known for the Tarikh-i-Firuz Shahi, a history of the sultanate to the early years of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/firuz-shah-tughluq">Firuz Shah Tughluq</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatwa">Fatwa</a>-i-Jahandari, advice for Muslim kings. Born at Baran (now Bulandshahr), he was for seventeen years a companion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-bin-tughluq">Muhammad bin Tughluq</a>. The Fatwa-i-Jahandari argued for a hierarchy of birth among Muslims, setting the ashraf upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">castes</a> apart from the ajlaf low castes, and urged kings to overpower and degrade the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindus</a>, while lamenting that the sultans in fact gave Hindus high posts. He fell out of favour after that sultan's death and died poor in 1357.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Barani came from an Indian Muslim family of Baran, which gave him his nisba; his ancestors had come there from Kaithal in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haryana">Haryana</a> but regarded Baran as their home. His family served the sultans of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> in high posts. His maternal grandfather Husam-ud-Din was an important officer of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balban">Balban</a>, his father Muwayyid-ul-Mulk was naib to Arkali Khan, son of Jalal-ud-Din Firuz Khalji, and his uncle Qazi Ala-ul-Mulk was kotwal, or police chief, of Delhi under Ala-ud-Din Khalji. Barani himself never held office. As a companion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-bin-tughluq">Muhammad bin Tughluq</a> he was close to the poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amir-khusrau">Amir Khusrau</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatwa">Fatwa</a>-i-Jahandari is written as advice to Muslim kings on how to earn religious merit and the gratitude of their subjects. It holds that religion and temporal government are twins. Scholars read its defence of aristocracy by birth differently: M. Athar Ali saw its model in Sassanid Iran rather than in race or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a>, while Muzaffar Alam argued that Barani chose it to serve the narrow interests of the early Muslim regime in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Barani also divided law into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sharia">Sharia</a> and the Zawabit, state laws made by the monarch with the nobility, which in his view had to keep to the spirit of the Sharia.</p><p>The Tarikh-i-Firuz Shahi, completed in 1357, covers the sultanate from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghiyath-al-din-tughluq">Ghiyath al-Din Tughluq</a> to the first six years of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/firuz-shah-tughluq">Firuz Shah Tughluq</a>. It presents the sultans who followed Barani's rules as succeeding and those who sinned as meeting their downfall. Because he did not consult contemporary works, his accounts of Ala-ud-Din Khalji's wars at Chittor, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranthambore-fort">Ranthambhor</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa">Malwa</a> and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malik-kafur">Malik Kafur</a>'s Deccan campaigns are sketchy. His other works include the Hasratnama and the Sana-i-Muhammadi.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-bin-tughluq">Muhammad bin Tughluq</a>'s death Barani wrote his two works on government, religion and history in the hope of winning the favour of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/firuz-shah-tughluq">Firuz Shah Tughluq</a>, but he received no reward. His gravestone lies in the courtyard of the dargah of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizamuddin-auliya">Nizamuddin Auliya</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>, near the tomb of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amir-khusrau">Amir Khusrau</a>. Later historians, among them Nizam-ud-Din Ahmad, Abd al-Qadir Badayuni and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/firishta">Firishta</a>, relied on his Tarikh for the period, and Abd al-Haqq al-Dehlawi drew on it for his lives of Nizam-ud-Din Auliya and other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> saints.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ziauddin Barani” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Strict chroniclers such as <b>Ziauddin Barani</b> urged harsher treatment of non-Muslims, and Firuz Shah acted against Shi’a and other sects he judged heretical.</blockquote><a href="/societies/delhi-sultanate">Social Hierarchy of the Delhi Sultanate</a></li></ul></section>
